00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.940 align:start position:0%
researchers have been covered evidence  of a Neolithic skull cult at an  important archaeological site in  southeastern Turkey they found skull  fragments with unique markings buried  among the earliest known examples of  monumental buildings sometimes called  the world's first temples since there  were markings on fragments from three  different skulls and they were found in  the context of the gobekli tepe site for  which a clear ritual component is  proposed researchers believe that these  fragments could be evidence of the  presence of a skull cult a skull cult  means people were modifying decorating  and displaying skulls in a ritual  context other decorated skulls been  found at other Neolithic sites in the  Middle East but this is the first such  find at Quebec Lea tempeh here the  bodies were buried the skulls  occasionally exhumed and stripped of any  remaining flesh subsequently skulls  could be decorated with paint or facial  features remodeled in clay analysis of  the new fragments revealed cut marks in  the skulls left over from the defleshing  process as well as deep grooves made  with Flint tools and puncture holes  these incisions and holes are a type of  skull modification that hasn't been seen  before in this region and suggests that  the skulls are strung up as decoration  it's too soon to tell whether these  skulls were marked as a way to honor  ancestors or punish fallen enemies  researchers will be back this fall to  look for more clues to the rituals more  skulls and the missing skeletons  [Music]
 

